{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do","id":927830,"title":"GMing Changeling: What NOT to do","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-07-07T16:32:58-07:00","id":927830,"title":"GMing Changeling: What NOT to do","content":"I saw a thread like this for Vampire the Masquerade, and since I was interesting in GMing a game of Changeling 2e, I was wondering: what are pitfalls to avoid gameplay-wise, what characters should not be brought out and what kind of general plots should not be done? I'm going to make mistakes, I know, but I'd rather not make rookie and or obvious ones - I tend to be oblivious to those.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-07T17:06:39-07:00","id":927842,"title":"","content":"(Readies notepad.) Still a newbie, myself, so I'll keep an eye on here. I do have some suspicions:<br /> <br /> 1) Don't let the players go rules lawyer on the Wyrd. (See: 1E Pledgecrafting abuses I've heard of) The Wyrd isn't a vending machine that gives out prizes for metagaming. It hates people who treat it as such.<br /> 2) Don't make the first threat obvious. Paranoia and suspense are important in Changeling. Build up before you reveal.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10118-bronzedog","name":"BronzeDog","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"239"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-07T18:02:24-07:00","id":927876,"title":"","content":"80% of the game is about building up home, doing things that root players in their sense of community and connection to the lives they are building.<br /> <br /> 50% of <i>the perception of the game</i> is about thinking the faeries are about to take them away again.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":14}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T05:19:24-07:00","id":928068,"title":"","content":"This is a thing in general, but especially Changeling--if you're going to play with any theme around sexual abuse, make sure you know what you're talking about. Spend hours (at least ten, unless you're a survivor or doctor who works with survivors) researching theeffects sexual violence can have, reading from survivors, etc. Also, make sure you check in with every single one of your players before you introduce something like that into the game. I'm serious about this, it's important.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:12px\">-</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":12}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3093-hm8453a","name":"hm8453a","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3093_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"636"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T07:04:59-07:00","id":928094,"title":"","content":"I'd stay away from introducing the theme of sexual abuse, but allow the players to introduce it themselves, if they so choose, that way it feels more natural and organic, and less artifically introduced.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2241,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","user":"Omegaphallic","date":". 07-08-2016, 10:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T07:18:43-07:00","id":928100,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>hm8453a</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928068#post928068\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is a thing in general, but especially Changeling--if you're going to play with any theme around sexual abuse, make sure you know what you're talking about. Spend hours (at least ten, unless you're a survivor or doctor who works with survivors) researching theeffects sexual violence can have, reading from survivors, etc. Also, make sure you check in with every single one of your players before you introduce something like that into the game. I'm serious about this, it's important.</div> </div> </div> </div> I'd be leering of going about things like that, you could go through a 100 sexual abuse statements and each will be unique in someway as will how they view the experience and deal with it. And you don't have anyway to tell which are true or not.<br /> <br /> And some experts do more harm then good and its hard to tell which is which.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2241-omegaphallic","name":"Omegaphallic","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T11:23:03-07:00","id":928293,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Omegaphallic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928100#post928100\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'd be leering of going about things like that, you could go through a 100 sexual abuse statements and each will be unique in someway as will how they view the experience and deal with it. And you don't have anyway to tell which are true or not.<br /> <br /> And some experts do more harm then good and its hard to tell which is which.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh, of course. Because we all react to trauma in different ways. But exposing yourself to some of the different ways we do recover will allow you to see that range, and ways to handle it delicately, rather than predatorily, or just for ~edge~. You also run the risk of hurting a player you might not have known is recovering from sexual abuse themselves.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-size:12px\">-</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3093-hm8453a","name":"hm8453a","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3093_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"636"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T11:46:07-07:00","id":928308,"title":"","content":"I was in the worst changeling game i ever experience in 10 years i have played RPGs.<br /> <br /> Nothing was right on that game and yet i never learn more about the why and hows of the game like in that one. Nothing teaches you about how a game works like seeing somebody screw it up to spectacularly.<br /> <br /> The DM decides that pledges were OP so all pledges could only be done via the pledge master. Shoving his annoying NPC down our throats.<br /> <br /> He decided that cannibalism feet ogres so all ogres were cannibals as a physiological necesity (which it isnt how the flaw works but whatever). But whenever any pc (freshly escaped ones btw) reacted badly to one of his NPCs ogres boasting how babies taste good then that was our pcs being intolerant.<br /> <br /> Allowed players to make character from 200+ years ago or that dont remember anything about their past lives so that left half the table playing normal changeling with the other half playing either murderhobbos or character so out of touch with human behavior that their only characteristic was &quot;Leelo Dallas multipass&quot;<br /> <br /> Make the courts leader horrible leaders (one with clarity 1, an inept, a moustache twirling arab terrorist.....literally he was that) but they were politically invincible because status quo was god and all the court said that they compete with each other but really nobody took advantage of nobody and a court could execute a member of the seasonally rulling court if they see it fit without consulting said rulling court.<br /> <br /> Artificially restricted skill to 2 only if your character background really REALLY justified and then limited your wyrd to 1 and then complain as to why noone wanted to go to his incredible dangerous quest in to the hedge to rescue 1 NPC that was a brooding samurai that nobody interacted with.<br /> <br /> Decide he didnt like how the contracts worked because wyrd was a godstat. But became lazy and doing only half of the ones player pick and rules on the moment how exactly do they work.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BronzeDog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=927842#post927842\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) Don't let the players go rules lawyer on the Wyrd. (See: 1E Pledgecrafting abuses I've heard of) The Wyrd isn't a vending machine that gives out prizes for metagaming. It hates people who treat it as such.</div> </div> </div> </div>Can i ask about this one?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T12:59:35-07:00","id":928360,"title":"","content":"Gonna second the &quot;don't have PCs from more than 80-ish years ago.&quot; From a ST perspective, it's really, really hard to engage those characters, as their worldview is so very removed. If you want to play stranger in a strange land, okay, but know that most of the game will be spent with &quot;What's this? What's this?&quot; about every single thing. <br /> <br /> Also seconding &quot;don't rules lawyer the Wyrd,&quot; particularly with pledges. One of the easiest things to do is to create a list of minor forbiddances and tasks (like, &quot;Don't eat blue ice cream on Mondays&quot; or &quot;Give a friend a dollar once a month&quot;), racking up a huge amount of benefits (because duration, the only other positive modifier, is capped at three). Or creating a life alert pledge with a forbiddance of &quot;don't fall unconcious due to damage&quot; and a sanction that auto-casts Eternal Spring 3 (heal target) and Direction 1 (find the target). Or the infinite pledge loop (step 1: get a boon of membership in a group, like Allies (somebody). step 2: Use Allies (somebody) as the mortal corporal for another pledge, which grants you Allies (somebody else). step 3: Repeat step 2. This one is a little less safe because if one breaks, it breaks down the chain, but it's kinda nuts.) Per RAW, these are legal, but the Wyrd should say, &quot;Yeah, no, those don't work like you want,&quot; either by causing creative interpretation (positive Contracts in a Sanction throw Dramatic Failures instead, circumstances prevent someone from having a dollar to give) or having the pledge not work.<br /> <br /> Other than that, watch the Catches to Contracts. Some are easy as sinning (barefoot for an hour? I don't know anyone that sleeps with their shoes on...), some are easily manipulated (&quot;Here, I'm going to give you this cupcake, then give it back to me.&quot; &quot;Okay?&quot; &quot;Great, now I can heal your bashing for free.&quot;), and some are never going to happen (an area dominated by lava? Not on this side of the crust!).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5695-aceofalmonds","name":"AceOfAlmonds","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5695_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"323"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T13:33:35-07:00","id":928388,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928308#post928308\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Can i ask about this one?</div> </div> </div> </div>In essence, pledges can solve all your mundane problems, that assumption is a carte blanche for the ST to complicate things for the characters regardless. It's like Unchained pacts without an ounce of the moral dilemma and way, way more versatile.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":576,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","user":"Ephsy","date":". 07-08-2016, 04:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T14:21:05-07:00","id":928421,"title":"","content":"Less than three days and already I'm getting advice I had no idea I'd be getting.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BronzeDog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=927842#post927842\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) Don't let the players go rules lawyer on the Wyrd. (See: 1E Pledgecrafting abuses I've heard of) The Wyrd isn't a vending machine that gives out prizes for metagaming. It hates people who treat it as such.<br /> 2) Don't make the first threat obvious. Paranoia and suspense are important in Changeling. Build up before you reveal.</div> </div> </div> </div>Always good to keep in mind; no point in throwing players into a universe only to suddenly claim that it's being threatened before they can tell if it's Sin City or not. Good note on Pledges too; I remember the 2e rules even mentioning that GMs could punish players for making such Pledges, though I imagine that punishments aren't something that should be given out too heavily either.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=927876#post927876\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">80% of the game is about building up home, doing things that root players in their sense of community and connection to the lives they are building.<br /> <br /> 50% of <i>the perception of the game</i> is about thinking the faeries are about to take them away again.</div> </div> </div> </div>I feel like this first one's going to be a pitfall for me, because I tend to go too in-depth with certain things. Still, as noted, it's always good to set up what kind of world the characters are establishing themselves in before a threat is brought up. Players are likely to work that much harder to get something that they have come to treasure, rather than something they just bought, if it's stolen from them.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3093-hm8453a\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3093_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3093\" data-vbnamecard=\"3093\">hm8453a</a> and @omegaphallic<br /> Very wise. Sexual abuse isn't a topic I'm all that comfortable with myself, nor knowledgeable, so bringing it up in-game just to &quot;spice things up&quot; is definitely something I'm going to avoid doing. Even from a non-interactive standpoint, sexual abuse is difficult to do well, and probably not within my skill as a writer to do.<br /> <br /> @LokiRavenSpeak<br /> You have my deepest sympathies for going through that kind of game. Even if it taught you well on what not to do, I don't envy you having to go through it. However, it does bring up a few good points. As the presence of this thread shows, I'm not experienced enough in the game to really be making judgement calls on modifying existing rules and contracts. I know other GMs who have done extensive hacks on the rules and setting, but only once they've determined what works and what doesn't, and they clear that with their players first. Likewise, good advice on how <i>not</i> to run a freehold - I'd think that any sensible leader, or at least their subordinates, would realize that having such low clarity meant they'd need to be taken out of the seat of power to get some therapy. Having political immunity and the ability to execute anyone on the streets sounds like a dangerous step towards Gentryhood to me.<br /> <br /> As for the restrictions on players I'm...honestly baffled. I can understand <i>recommending</i> players spreading their points out for skills, as it means they won't be hit with as many penalties, but limiting them <i>and</i> their Wyrd? At that point it's almost better to just give them pre-made characters.<br /> <br /> For the age-point, I agree to a point, though I do feel there is some genuine character drama that can come from someone who is far from their original time period. It would require a fair amount of work from both the GM and the player to make sure it wasn't a gimmick that got old fast. At the very least, taking a contract like &quot;Inanimate Communion&quot; or having a Token which informs the user about unfamiliar things seems like a way to mitigate things...but yeah, definitely something to be careful about.<br /> <br /> @AceOfDiamonds<br /> All good points, particularly on the Pledges and Catches. From what I've seen, 2e has made the Catches for various contracts much easier to manage and more situational the higher a contract is rated, but keeping track of what contract needs what is a good bit of advice all the same. That said, giving some opportunities for the players to find/make conditions for their contracts to be ennacted wouldn't be a bad idea - in a Changeling game I'm in, we have a Beast character who has Cyclopean Strength, and he used the Satiety Expenditure effect to allow him to effectively throw people around. He threw my Changeling character (he is an Airtouched, so he can take no damage from falls below terminal velocity) after someone who was kidnapping the local freehold leader, which generated enough wind around my Changeling to allow him to activate Contract of the Elements: Wind with its catch. It was a fun time. :-)<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ephsy</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928388#post928388\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> In essence, pledges can solve all your mundane problems, that assumption is a carte blanche for the ST to complicate things for the characters regardless. It's like Unchained pacts without an ounce of the moral dilemma and way, way more versatile.</div> </div> </div> </div>Now that I was unaware of...I'll need to re-read Pledges for 2e to see how concurrent this definition is.<br /> <br /> Thanks to everyone who has posted, and who posts in the future (hoping that this remains as a resource for others GMing Changeling in the future).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T14:58:44-07:00","id":928456,"title":"","content":"2e pledges are WAY better. 1e was a cool idea but the math was really wonky and abusable, and didn't make sense in a lot of places. Particularly Forbiddance and Duration. It's a hell of a lot easier to keep a chastity pledge for a day than it is to keep it for a year, but because Duration is a positive modifier the shorter the time you pledged to Not Do X the more you benefitted from Not Doing X.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T16:05:54-07:00","id":928484,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Taidragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928421#post928421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">@LokiRavenSpeak<br /> You have my deepest sympathies for going through that kind of game. Even if it taught you well on what not to do, I don't envy you having to go through it. However, it does bring up a few good points. As the presence of this thread shows, I'm not experienced enough in the game to really be making judgement calls on modifying existing rules and contracts. I know other GMs who have done extensive hacks on the rules and setting, but only once they've determined what works and what doesn't, and they clear that with their players first.</div> </div> </div> </div>While i dont mind houserules the problem was that this DM allow us, changeling veterans, to make &quot;experieced pcs&quot; but them he spring this houserules/setting changes which changed fundamentally how the game work as surprises so it resulted in my character telling the pledge master to go to hell and then &quot;remembering&quot; that he was the only one who could make pledges for a motley.<br /> <br /> Interestingly enough i never got the point of motleys until i play this game and after reading in frustration the motely pledge it says that each member have to help each other meaning that a changeling trading an i-owe-you token is not just pulling himself but his entire motley who are pledge-sworn to help with this which is a political power in its own right.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Taidragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928421#post928421\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Likewise, good advice on how <i>not</i> to run a freehold - I'd think that any sensible leader, or at least their subordinates, would realize that having such low clarity meant they'd need to be taken out of the seat of power to get some therapy. Having political immunity and the ability to execute anyone on the streets sounds like a dangerous step towards Gentryhood to me.</div> </div> </div> </div>It was weird, the seeds of plot were there. Winter king was mad in the sense of the queen of hearts in alice and while he was otherwordly beautiful and i could see some people ignoring that aspect of him. Not all of them, when he lost control to a newly escaped changeling`s perceive insult (which said changeling went to a long lenght to explain and apologize) and almost had his champion execute her. None of the NPCs reacted, it was like that was usual monday.<br /> <br /> Same with the DM version of Summer Queen which was in all but name subservient to the mad king of winter.....and nobody in summer cared about that even though their personalities would dictate that they should care. The change in station was so pointless because they all got along by the power of &quot;just ignore&quot;.<br /> <br /> My advice to make a freehold is that if you want, and its perfectly okay if you dont, politicking in your freehold there has to be dissidence and you have to show that dissidence to the players. It can be as extreme as covert revolution or as subtle as whisper and bitching while drinking. It can be a guy who openly opposes the monarch in peaceful but firm way or it can be a guy who is valuable to the monarch but know how she/he is and gives tips to the players to survive around the monarch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T16:29:00-07:00","id":928497,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928484#post928484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interestingly enough i never got the point of motleys until i play this game and after reading in frustration the motely pledge it says that each member have to help each other meaning that a changeling trading an i-owe-you token is not just pulling himself but his entire motley who are pledge-sworn to help with this which is a political power in its own right.</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh. You know, that makes a lot of sense. It never occurred to me to think like that.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928484#post928484\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It was weird, the seeds of plot were there. Winter king was mad in the sense of the queen of hearts in alice and while he was otherwordly beautiful and i could see some people ignoring that aspect of him. Not all of them, when he lost control to a newly escaped changeling`s perceive insult (which said changeling went to a long lenght to explain and apologize) and almost had his champion execute her. None of the NPCs reacted, it was like that was usual monday.<br /> <br /> Same with the DM version of Summer Queen which was in all but name subservient to the mad king of winter.....and nobody in summer cared about that even though their personalities would dictate that they should care. The change in station was so pointless because they all got along by the power of &quot;just ignore&quot;.<br /> <br /> My advice to make a freehold is that if you want, and its perfectly okay if you dont, politicking in your freehold there has to be dissidence and you have to show that dissidence to the players. It can be as extreme as covert revolution or as subtle as whisper and bitching while drinking. It can be a guy who openly opposes the monarch in peaceful but firm way or it can be a guy who is valuable to the monarch but know how she/he is and gives tips to the players to survive around the monarch.</div> </div> </div> </div>Considering I'm making my own Freehold, complete with custom courts, contracts and courtiers, that is extremely valuable advice. Thank you. :-)<br /> <br /> You know, with the way that things were going, it almost seems like your DM was going towards making the freehold become an Arcadia on earth, potentially with the Winter king as the gentry of it. I'm starting to form a bile fascination with this session; I imagine it'd have been more interesting if it wasn't being played straight.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T19:51:52-07:00","id":928563,"title":"","content":"A thing which tangentially relates to Loki's GM's cannibalism gaffe and more directly relates to one of those common reading attitudes about the game:<br /> <br /> 2e's done a good job at making it more up-front, and Arc's old spiel about Never Going Back as the corollary to Finding Your Way Home is pretty direct about it, but it bears emphasis that <b>the Durance and its related traumas do not render the Lost immune to casual malice</b>.<br /> <br /> Even beyond the explicit &quot;bad guy&quot; narrative archetypes that the Wyrd can and does orchestrate, most changelings who've been around for a while are not going to be especially good people without deliberate effort. This is a group of people with multiple innate means of getting out of trouble and a natural bias toward a smaller-scale version of the same behavior as the beings that made them into magical-narrative actors that can talk to the whole world. The vast majority have learned better than to do anything for free, and no small number of them have deeply-ingrained agendas amplified by the loss of their emotional regulators.<br /> <br /> Put plainly, don't run changeling NPCs as simply humans who happen to be able to call on ancient pixie pacts, because that misses out on the ways that the Lost are fae.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":12}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T20:12:51-07:00","id":928569,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Taidragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928497#post928497\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You know, with the way that things were going, it almost seems like your DM was going towards making the freehold become an Arcadia on earth, potentially with the Winter king as the gentry of it. I'm starting to form a bile fascination with this session; I imagine it'd have been more interesting if it wasn't being played straight.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not quite what the DM had in mind. The game had its big reveal of where it was going until said revelation made the game all but implode by the sheer number of DM fiat needed for that plot to work.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> So as i said before the main plot was the dissapearance of brooding samurai who was the champion of spring who was an NPC who appeared in 1 session and he only got a tangible relationship with 1 of the PCs. Then the broodmurai was lost in hedge during a rescue and nobody follow that plot point because the GM artificially restricted our advancement as i mentioned earlier and it was all but a chance die or 1 die to navigate the edge. And as nobody knew the guy, nobody really care that much to risk it.<br /> <br /> So barely any time passed and someone assassinated the spring queen and all the courtiers, my character a detective darkling Gravewight whose ONLY contracts were the shade and spirit ones ask the corpse as about who kill him. The dm sudenly decided that my power didnt work like it said in the book because it ruined the dramatism so without any other clue and mimes-privateers (yes, i am serious) attacking that plot was also shelved because pixel bitching.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Lesson here, you have to take into account the types of stories you can and you cannot do with a game. For example unless backed up by another mage a mundane murder mystery is all but resolved in 2 minutes by a mage. Same here, if there is a detective gravewreight with 5 in shade and spirit then a murder mystery shouldnt be the meat of the story. A another kind of mystery sure. The complications of solving the murder mystery? Sure. But the murder mystery itself? Hardly.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> So the spring court dissipated and the new spring court was given to the mustache twirling Muslim terrorist, this is not an exaggeration he was a Muslim terrorist who was replaced by a fetch just when the USA government was about to kill him with drones. Bootleg Bin Laden, was appointed spring king and he proceed to openly plan and execute a plan to blow up an hospital full of people to kill 1 Fae that from time to time came to steal people. As usual in that game neither the NPCs or the PCs who were friends with the DM reacted badly or better put, reacted at all to that because the DM interpreted clarity as the most permissive path of enlightenment ala masquerade.<br /> <br /> The ice king just got wyrd 10 and went to arcadia, kill his fae and took its place as a lord of arcadia that was all. We were told he was a example of a mad ruler.......except that when everyone around you doesnt react to your madness nobody oppose him and whenever anyone even voice concern about the things he was doing a NPC would tell you nicely &quot;that kind of talk was gonna get you kill&quot;.<br /> <br /> So finally the Broodmurai came back and vomit the rest of the plot. The plot was that one of the underling of the Spring king was kill off by one of his courtiers (who was a maniac laughing woodman who had all but tattooed &quot;i molest people as hobby&quot; written on his forehead) he left a fake body behind and went to the edge and capture the broodmurai in a pledge as a slave. Remember when i said that the DM had rule that pledges could only be made with the pledge master? Well that doesn't applied when it gets in the way of <br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <img src=\"https://cdn.meme.am/instances/500x/69333686.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> <br /> He really, really loves that word.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> So he took the broodmurai to the goblin market and use his abilities (he was a ice elemental) to mass produce ice rays for the goblins (yes, i know that one cannot just mass produce tokens by raw) and he was planning to attack the freehold with the goblins from the goblin market armed with ice rays to sell everyone to the goblins to make more ray weapons.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Yup, that was the big plot. Ice rays, goblins with ice rays. As you can see by now i havent mentioned what happened to either the summer queen or the autumn king, well that because.<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> The didn't matter....at all. Summer queen was all but subservient to the winter king because he was &quot;charming&quot; and all her court just ignore how she was failing and failing hard to do what summer is suppose to do. She was super bad-ass ex navy seal and the dm idea or her was somewhat similar of that over idealization of the army that Hideo Kojima has.....that i can forgive of him because Hideo is just a army otaku and doesn't know any better while the DM was in the actual navy and he should know by now that the navy is not entirely compose of a ultra patriotic warrior monk who sweat in red, white and blue. <br /> <br /> And autumn never do anything because the DM read in the core they were secretive and so their king only appeared on the changing of crown relevants to him, and he (the dm) decided to transplant the idea of winter that you have to wait a year to be accepted into the court.....he decide that had to be a year IRL. So by the end the game he complained as to why nobody wanted to join autumn.<br /> </div></div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Lesson here, perception trumps reality any day. If the players feel something (be that a plot, faction, etc) is impenetrable then it might as well be that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T20:38:11-07:00","id":928577,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928563#post928563\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A thing which tangentially relates to Loki's GM's cannibalism gaffe and more directly relates to one of those common reading attitudes about the game:<br /> <br /> 2e's done a good job at making it more up-front, and Arc's old spiel about Never Going Back as the corollary to Finding Your Way Home is pretty direct about it, but it bears emphasis that <b>the Durance and its related traumas do not render the Lost immune to casual malice</b>.<br /> <br /> Even beyond the explicit &quot;bad guy&quot; narrative archetypes that the Wyrd can and does orchestrate, most changelings who've been around for a while are not going to be especially good people without deliberate effort. This is a group of people with multiple innate means of getting out of trouble and a natural bias toward a smaller-scale version of the same behavior as the beings that made them into magical-narrative actors that can talk to the whole world. The vast majority have learned better than to do anything for free, and no small number of them have deeply-ingrained agendas amplified by the loss of their emotional regulators.<br /> <br /> Put plainly, don't run changeling NPCs as simply humans who happen to be able to call on ancient pixie pacts, because that misses out on the ways that the Lost are fae.</div> </div> </div> </div>Though i have some issue with the questions that raises Arcs speech about never going back, specially the example that when you are starving on the Hedge stealing stops becoming a sin which by itself raises the question of how, if that kind of act gives you an entire new system of morality then why a starving child in LA who steals food doesnt get his own special morality and just his reduced? But i will probably be better explored in the book when its release.<br /> <br /> But more on the bad guy narritive and changeling are not simply humans. My DM took this to the other extreme and make every NPC a gentry but in name. The problem wasnt that the NPC was a cannibal or even that he was casual and explicit about it but the feedback it got.<br /> <br /> He was the face of a court and he grabbed a bunch of freshly escaped changeling and casually commented that he was gonna eat a baby later in the day, this wasnt him provoking a reaction or him just joking, he was just telling it on the same tone one say &quot;im gonna grab a pint on the pub later&quot; and when one of the PC reacted by being weirded out, the DM framed the scene as the PC was discriminating the ogre. With the ogre getting on the face of the PC like that was racial insult to him<br /> <br /> Like i tell my players, if you wanna play a cannibal and be public and gleeful about it go ahead but keep in mind you will be only be like by a select group of people because society (even changeling society) wont react well to the guy who eats babies. So, at least in 1E, being a public cannibal is gonna make you a social pariah and if you live decades outside of arcadia (like that NPC) then you have to be used to people not reacting well when you comment on your cannibalism.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 07-08-2016, 11:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T23:16:14-07:00","id":928611,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928577#post928577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Though i have some issue with the questions that raises Arcs speech about never going back, specially the example that when you are starving on the Hedge stealing stops becoming a sin which by itself raises the question of how, if that kind of act gives you an entire new system of morality then why a starving child in LA who steals food doesnt get his own special morality and just his reduced? But i will probably be better explored in the book when its release.</div> </div> </div> </div>I am confused about what is being said here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T23:54:20-07:00","id":928619,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928611#post928611\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I am confused about what is being said here.</div> </div> </div> </div>Elsewhere you said this, 'Freeholds have to protect their citizens from each other because of this risk of 'sudden eruption', particularly in a world that encourages. Hell, stop and consider, for a second, the Hedge. One of it's biggest qualities is that it forces people who enter to reconsider their values. Think stealing is a sin? After weeks of starvation in the Hedge, will you still think that when you see a goblin leaves his stall unattended? That's more or less a straight up example from the book of what the Hedge does to people. And that's just one of several forces that tug and pull on any given changeling.'<br /> <br /> How is this changeling starving and stealing food from a goblin any different from a human starving and stealing food from another human?<br /> <br /> Presumably the making of Breaking Points from the five questions is being overlooked and few people have 'Stealing food when I'm starving' as the worst thing they could imagine themselves doing. But LokiRavenSpeak notes it will probably be in the book.<br /> <br /> Just an attempt to translate, feel free to direct responses back to LokiRavenSpeak providing I've gotten it right.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-09T00:05:55-07:00","id":928621,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928619#post928619\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Elsewhere you said this, 'Freeholds have to protect their citizens from each other because of this risk of 'sudden eruption', particularly in a world that encourages. Hell, stop and consider, for a second, the Hedge. One of it's biggest qualities is that it forces people who enter to reconsider their values. Think stealing is a sin? After weeks of starvation in the Hedge, will you still think that when you see a goblin leaves his stall unattended? That's more or less a straight up example from the book of what the Hedge does to people. And that's just one of several forces that tug and pull on any given changeling.'<br /> <br /> How is this changeling starving and stealing food from a goblin any different from a human starving and stealing food from another human?<br /> <br /> Presumably the making of Breaking Points from the five questions is being overlooked and few people have 'Stealing food when I'm starving' as the worst thing they could imagine themselves doing. But LokiRavenSpeak notes it will probably be in the book.<br /> <br /> Just an attempt to translate, feel free to direct responses back to LokiRavenSpeak providing I've gotten it right.</div> </div> </div> </div>If that is the case, then I find it worth noting that what I am referencing is the Hedge's tendency to position travellers and inhabitants into positions that force them to reconsider their viewpoints, not positing that one is worse than the other somehow. The Hedge is one of many factors that keeps the Fae from settling on anything, because their perception is always changing.<br /> <br /> THe big way this is important is that Changelings are constantly shifting creatures. A noble defender of the freehold who swears one day that he will never betray or abandon anyone to the Strangers will, almost invariably, find the limits of that testament and do just that. There's no eternity to outlook in Lost, or at least it's not safe to assume there is such.<br /> <br /> Trust is hard, in Changeling, because the fairies are fickle, and most people you deal with are fairies. You can't just assume your trust will last forever-you have to fight to keep it from changing away.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-09T01:09:53-07:00","id":928635,"title":"","content":"General advice... Don't do what your players don't like in a campaign.<br /> <br /> If they don't like the pervasive darkness of Changeling, then don't do that. Do a fantasy campaign. If they don't want a fantasy jaunt through a fantastical world, try focusing on humanity, maybe the characters trying to connect with their family, or just humans in general, in the aftermath of being hideously mutated. If they don't want that... Throw in Vampires. They're strangely versatile as a crossover monster if you think of them as &quot;guardians of the masquerade&quot;...","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/873174-dragon-rekindled-the-orobori-chronicles-embers-reboot#post873174\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"font-size:14px\"><span style=\"font-family:georgia\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\"><b>Dragon Rekindled: The Oroboroi Chronicles.</b></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#696969\">The official reboot of <b>Dragon: The Embers</b>.</span></span></span></a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10120-rockstarraccoon","name":"RockstarRaccoon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10120_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"189"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-09T01:40:49-07:00","id":928642,"title":"","content":"Building on that to some degree: seek to understand why your players don't like a thing. And if that thing seems really important to the game, try and figure out the cross purposes. Maybe it can be worked out.<br /> <br /> And remember: no game can be for all people at all times.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":7}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-09T09:27:13-07:00","id":928761,"title":"","content":"To add on to this, and a bit of what Loki said earlier, take note of what kiths/contracts/merits the players take. Those are the powers they want to use, and will be the first thing they look at for solutions to any problems you introduce. Changeling has a wide variety of powers, some of which bypass certain types of problems entirely. If one of your players has Phantom Glory, for example, they're just going to ghost through most physical obstacles. That's not necessarily a bad thing, but you'll want to provide problems that can be easily solved by the players' skillsets so that they can feel competent and useful, and problems that they don't have an easy out for to make them worry and think, and that's a loooot easier to do if you have a handle on how their powers work.<br /> <br /> This goes double for combat: it's a major subsystem and it works just differently enough from the rest of play to cause some serious dissonance if you aren't aware of it. The specifics of why could easily be their own essay but I'll leave that for later in favor of a specific example: My first Changeling character was a Summer Court MetalFlesh/FireHeart. I was looking to play an angry tanky type, and without really coordinating with the rest of the party I just picked things that seemed effective and thematically appropriate. I did too good a job, and it wasn't long before we realized that anything that could deal more than a single point of damage to me would kill anyone else in the Motley in two hits at most. This left the ST really struggling to provide fights that were interesting for me and not insanely lethal for everyone else.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-09T12:20:00-07:00","id":928859,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928621#post928621\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If that is the case, then I find it worth noting that what I am referencing is the Hedge's tendency to position travellers and inhabitants into positions that force them to reconsider their viewpoints, not positing that one is worse than the other somehow. The Hedge is one of many factors that keeps the Fae from settling on anything, because their perception is always changing.<br /> <br /> THe big way this is important is that Changelings are constantly shifting creatures. A noble defender of the freehold who swears one day that he will never betray or abandon anyone to the Strangers will, almost invariably, find the limits of that testament and do just that. There's no eternity to outlook in Lost, or at least it's not safe to assume there is such.<br /> <br /> Trust is hard, in Changeling, because the fairies are fickle, and most people you deal with are fairies. You can't just assume your trust will last forever-you have to fight to keep it from changing away.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Pretty much what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar886_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"886\" data-vbnamecard=\"886\">nofather</a> said, when i read the thread the tangent of the conversation was at the time about the clarity so i understood that what you were saying in there was about the process in which a changeling lost integrity and gained clarity which wasnt the case.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-09T14:55:40-07:00","id":928909,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928859#post928859\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Pretty much what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar886_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"886\" data-vbnamecard=\"886\">nofather</a> said, when i read the thread the tangent of the conversation was at the time about the clarity so i understood that what you were saying in there was about the process in which a changeling lost integrity and gained clarity which wasnt the case.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, it was not about the process of a changeling losing Integrity and gaining Clarity. That's a fuzz point. It was about how the faerie world will basically manipulate you into confronting your worldviews and opinions, particularly your &quot;I will nots&quot;, in a such a way that you either break, change, or reveal a deeper monster. The argument was about how changelings aren't trustworthy, how just because you're all victims together does not mean you are necessarily safe with them or that the end line is that they will always fight for one another, and what that means for freeholds.<br /> <br /> When all that glitters, even the stars, and all that's precious, even your life, can be stolen by the fairy world in the blink of an eye, it's not safe to assume the values you hold one day will remain the next. And if it's not safe to assume that of yourself, it's <i>certainly</i> not safe to assume it of others.<br /> <br /> <i>Changelings are not safe to be around.</i>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 07-10-2016, 01:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-04T11:12:35-07:00","id":943729,"title":"","content":"Don't use Huntsmen. Just ignore everything that involves them. They're an unfun addition to the game, much like the other 2e antagonists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6783-notanautomaton","name":"notanautomaton","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-04T11:42:46-07:00","id":943750,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>notanautomaton</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=943729#post943729\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Don't use Huntsmen. Just ignore everything that involves them. They're an unfun addition to the game, much like the other 2e antagonists.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Hmm, I'm not sure this is help to the original poster given the aim of the thread, as your advocating cutting a core part of the game based on an incredibly subjective reading. Maybe going into what you think would be a funner alternative for him and his group instead of Huntsmen, and just keeping your first sentence, would have been better. That would give him an example to draw from and maybe inspire him to look at the material in a new way. Also;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>notanautomaton</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=943729#post943729\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> They're an unfun addition to the game, much like the other 2e antagonists.</div> </div> </div> </div>Just feels like its unnecessarily trying to start an argument that might derail the thread especially being that subjective, so might be better to edit that out. I apologize if I'm seeming nitpicky or targeting you, just trying to give advice in line with what I see being the goal of the thread and based off of how I've seen other threads tilt towards becoming arguments among posters.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1705,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1705-okami","user":"Okami","date":". 08-04-2016, 02:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1705-okami","name":"Okami","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1705_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"152"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-04T12:41:37-07:00","id":943785,"title":"","content":"&quot;Unfun&quot; strikes me as a useless word for these discussions, especially since we're talking about a horror game. Roleplaying games in general aren't necessarily about having a lighthearted good time. Extra Credits uses the term &quot;engagement&quot; for what many of us are really after. We want characters, settings, and events that are worth caring about. We want to fear for our characters' lives and sanity when they encounter danger. We want to share in their struggles, triumphs, and despair. We want to see how their world changes as we make decisions.<br /> <br /> If Huntsmen don't look like they'd be engaging antagonists, explain what the problem is. While there are some poorly designed game elements out there in RPG land, many are just easily misused or misunderstood.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10118-bronzedog","name":"BronzeDog","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"239"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-04T14:08:04-07:00","id":943856,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BronzeDog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=943785#post943785\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Unfun&quot; strikes me as a useless word for these discussions, especially since we're talking about a horror game. Roleplaying games in general aren't necessarily about having a lighthearted good time. Extra Credits uses the term &quot;engagement&quot; for what many of us are really after. We want characters, settings, and events that are worth caring about. We want to fear for our characters' lives and sanity when they encounter danger. We want to share in their struggles, triumphs, and despair. We want to see how their world changes as we make decisions.<br /> <br /> If Huntsmen don't look like they'd be engaging antagonists, explain what the problem is. While there are some poorly designed game elements out there in RPG land, many are just easily misused or misunderstood.</div> </div> </div> </div>You can't get rid of them once they twig onto you except by skipping town or giving up another Changeling. (You could also rob another Changeling for their token thingy, but that comes up with a different problem, which I'll elaborate on later.) Neither of those is a fun thing to do, and the first is functionally equivalent to character death. So if the players are unwilling to do the second, you're stuck running 'Huntsman attacks' stories for the rest of the game, until someone quits. <br /> <br /> They also distort the fabric of Changeling society, making Changelings regularly give each other up. Changelings used to come together to fight the fae and support each other, now they come together to more easily stab each other in the back. In 1e only Loyalists gave up their fellows, now everyone does it, which isn't what made Changeling society engaging.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6783-notanautomaton","name":"notanautomaton","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-04T14:30:40-07:00","id":943883,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>notanautomaton</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=943856#post943856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You can't get rid of them once they twig onto you except by skipping town or giving up another Changeling. (You could also rob another Changeling for their token thingy, but that comes up with a different problem, which I'll elaborate on later.) Neither of those is a fun thing to do, and the first is functionally equivalent to character death. So if the players are unwilling to do the second, you're stuck running 'Huntsman attacks' stories for the rest of the game, until someone quits.<br /> <br /> They also distort the fabric of Changeling society, making Changelings regularly give each other up. Changelings used to come together to fight the fae and support each other, now they come together to more easily stab each other in the back. In 1e only Loyalists gave up their fellows, now everyone does it, which isn't what made Changeling society engaging.</div> </div> </div> </div>Considering that a number of the rituals in the sample courts (primarily the Iron Court) have rituals involving hunting Huntsmen, it seems that killing them is a valid option as well, possibly with some permanence. I've not read anything that suggest Changelings regularly give each other up to prevent Huntsmen from coming after them, particularly since that method only delays them for a time. Also aren't there two merits in Mantle specifically designed to help prevent Huntsmen from coming after Changelings?<br /> <br /> I get what you're saying, I do, but I feel that this may be an overreaction to something we've not gotten all the information for (and that information is in itself a first draft).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-04T16:37:38-07:00","id":943981,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>notanautomaton</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=943856#post943856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You can't get rid of them once they twig onto you except by skipping town or giving up another Changeling. (You could also rob another Changeling for their token thingy, but that comes up with a different problem, which I'll elaborate on later.) Neither of those is a fun thing to do, and the first is functionally equivalent to character death. So if the players are unwilling to do the second, you're stuck running 'Huntsman attacks' stories for the rest of the game, until someone quits.<br /> <br /> They also distort the fabric of Changeling society, making Changelings regularly give each other up. Changelings used to come together to fight the fae and support each other, now they come together to more easily stab each other in the back. In 1e only Loyalists gave up their fellows, now everyone does it, which isn't what made Changeling society engaging.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not that Changelings regularly give each other up, it's about maintaining the possibility that someone might turn you over. The purpose of the tokens of reprieve isn't just to get Changelings to betray each other, but to make trust that much harder. For every Changeling who actually does make the deal, there are probably a thousand potential partnerships that didn't form or fell apart for fear one would betray the other. I don't see that as a big change: The more threatened a Changeling feels by their Keeper, the more the temptation grows. Trust was already difficult because just about anyone could be a Loyalist or coerced into betrayal. The Huntsmen are just another antagonist that uses that fear, to go alongside Loyalists and Privateers. An important difference is that they have rules about approaches that traitor Changelings aren't bound by.<br /> <br /> IIRC, there's also already one way mentioned for getting rid of a Huntsman permanently: Turn their Arcadian Weapons back on them, and it permakills that Huntsman. There are probably more we haven't seen, as Taidragon says.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10118-bronzedog","name":"BronzeDog","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"239"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-04T21:39:22-07:00","id":944123,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BronzeDog</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=943981#post943981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not that Changelings regularly give each other up, it's about maintaining the possibility that someone might turn you over. The purpose of the tokens of reprieve isn't just to get Changelings to betray each other, but to make trust that much harder. For every Changeling who actually does make the deal, there are probably a thousand potential partnerships that didn't form or fell apart for fear one would betray the other. I don't see that as a big change: The more threatened a Changeling feels by their Keeper, the more the temptation grows. Trust was already difficult because just about anyone could be a Loyalist or coerced into betrayal. The Huntsmen are just another antagonist that uses that fear, to go alongside Loyalists and Privateers. An important difference is that they have rules about approaches that traitor Changelings aren't bound by.<br /> <br /> IIRC, there's also already one way mentioned for getting rid of a Huntsman permanently: Turn their Arcadian Weapons back on them, and it permakills that Huntsman. There are probably more we haven't seen, as Taidragon says.</div> </div> </div> </div>But that possibility always existed, and making it explicit just makes it worse. <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Taidragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=943883#post943883\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Considering that a number of the rituals in the sample courts (primarily the Iron Court) have rituals involving hunting Huntsmen, it seems that killing them is a valid option as well, possibly with some permanence. I've not read anything that suggest Changelings regularly give each other up to prevent Huntsmen from coming after them, particularly since that method only delays them for a time. Also aren't there two merits in Mantle specifically designed to help prevent Huntsmen from coming after Changelings?<br /> <br /> I get what you're saying, I do, but I feel that this may be an overreaction to something we've not gotten all the information for (and that information is in itself a first draft).</div> </div> </div> </div>If you kill them they just show up again. Unless we get something to the effect of 'Huntsmen die when they are killed' it'll still be a problem.<br /> <br /> And in the draft it says that a significant portion of Changelings have the tokens that they get from giving up other Changelings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6783-notanautomaton","name":"notanautomaton","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-04T22:40:55-07:00","id":944140,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>notanautomaton</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=944123#post944123\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But that possibility always existed, and making it explicit just makes it worse.<br /> <br /> If you kill them they just show up again. Unless we get something to the effect of 'Huntsmen die when they are killed' it'll still be a problem.<br /> <br /> And in the draft it says that a significant portion of Changelings have the tokens that they get from giving up other Changelings.</div> </div> </div> </div>Significant doesn't mean have to mean &quot;massive number&quot; or &quot;more than half&quot;; it means that there's just a larger number than Changelings would be comfortable with. 1 percent in the scale of Unemployment is significant, even if it's comparing 4 percent to 5 percent. You are the GM: define what &quot;significant&quot; means in your game. Also note that while Changelings are constantly <i>paranoid</i> of their Gentry coming for them, it doesn't mean they <i>will</i>. The GM is the one who decides just how many of the characters are missed by Huntsmen, and how long it takes for a Huntsman to return. Just because killing a Huntsman may not ensure it won't come back, it doesn't mean it will <i>immediately</i> come back.<br /> <br /> I also will repeat myself from before: we are working from an incomplete first draft. There is precedent for one to make additional rules if not alter existing ones for better balance. We don't even really have the stats for Huntsmen weapons as of yet, even though they are a weakness of theirs and a blow reduces clarity in Changelings. If they seem too powerful, weaken them.<br /> <br /> However, if you don't like them there's no need to use them. There's plenty a Changeling needs to worry about from Arcadia aside from Huntsmen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":5803,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","user":"Taidragon","date":". 08-05-2016, 01:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-05T03:46:57-07:00","id":944219,"title":"","content":"Huntsmen can be killed permanently. The exact mechanics have not been spoiled yet, but it involves turning their Arcadian weapons on them. What's more, killing one permanently means that the scrap of Changeling used to create is no longer available to the Fae that made it. You can, very explicitly, permanently remove a Huntsman from play.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-05T10:52:49-07:00","id":944411,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>notanautomaton</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=944123#post944123\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But that possibility always existed, and making it explicit just makes it worse.</div> </div> </div> </div>I might be misreading your intent, but I'm assuming you mean the possibility of being turned over to their Keeper by a traitor Changeling. Yes, the possibility existed in 1e. I thought it was explicit before, and I don't see how making it more explicit changes the game.<br /> <br /> Changelings are supposed to be wary to the point of paranoia about being betrayed. I've always seen that internal conflict of wanting to band together in a united community versus the fear of betrayal as a central part of Changeling psychology. It's a major theme, and having Huntsmen offer Tokens of Reprieve serves to reinforce it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10118-bronzedog","name":"BronzeDog","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"239"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-06T08:43:48-07:00","id":944868,"title":"","content":"What NOT to do?<br /> <br /> Quite simply--don't overprepare. The Wyrd is a fickle and strange force that latches onto the smallest and weirdest phrasing of a promise and enacts it as ALL-CONSUMING TRUTH. A little bullshitting here and a little seat-of-your-pants there fits in a lot better than plot-for-days. I personally took the Dark Souls route and filled out most of the important lore with written mini-articles between sessions, usually excerpts from books the PCs found or otherwise had access to. When it came to the actual sessions, though, overpreparing usually just made the session too confusing.<br /> <br /> You can't be too weird with the Wyrd, if you pardon the pun. In one of my chronicle's most climactic moments, a character wrested control of the Wyrd itself, and I asked his player to redefine initiative, attack rolls, defense totals, etc. for the scene. (A wimpy character utterly destroyed an enemy by punching with Manipulation + Subterfuge.) Afterwards, I decided his Clarity dropped to zero, but as a Darkling, that triggered his Blessing and he kept 1 in the end. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> When the players defeated a low-level True Fae, and thus captured its Titles, by learning its true name, I granted them additional Wyrd at a Clarity cost. This all works in the CtL setting, but wouldn't AT ALL work in any another setting.<br /> <br /> Something that worked rather well, surprisingly, was intra-party conflict or deception. The fairy bastards are inherently untrustworthy, and that can work to a chronicle's benefit. Most of my game's PCs (and players) had huge secrets from each other, and those that didn't were still paranoid. Paranoia is important to CtL, as others have said.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8335-pondrthis","name":"pondrthis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-08-13T20:31:20-07:00","id":950407,"title":"","content":"Adding to this with my own ancedote: Make sure everyone's on the same page as to what the terminology is. I've been starting up a Skype game of Changeling with a few friends, and unfortunately one of the guys, who was just learning the game, thought Fetches were familiars, causing my co-GM and I to be blindsided about the fact that he placed a <i>bear</i> as his character's Fetch. Fortunately he was able to fix this blunder quickly, but still.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11755-aquatica1000","name":"Aquatica1000","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11755_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"31"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-07-02T23:50:52-07:00","id":1232056,"title":"","content":"Reviving this topic with a few examples from my most recent Changeling game. There were fun things done, but this dragged me out of the experience.<br /> <br /> If your players have established themselves as having a pretty important job for the freehold, such as hosting a halfway house for newly escaped Changelings, do not establish a very important plot where the players are working in place of one of the court monarchs. If you do, make sure that it does not need to be executed within a relatively tight schedule at the very beginning of the game. Let them invest time to their job, get a norm going, build up some ancillary skills, and <i>then </i>throw in things that they need to be concerned with on a tight schedule. Any invested in the job, if not built for it, will find themselves disconcerted when they're put out of their element, and considering everyone is only at Wyrd 1 at that point it will force them to focus their XP solely on preparing for said plot and not on actually developing their characters.<br /> <br /> Related, if you see/hear a player badgering another how to spend their own experiences, stop it. Some guidance is good, but outright telling someone how to spend it and making them question their own character is very much not okay. A person's character is their own creation. Suggestions are fine, but if you're going to take the reigns of their development then you are going way too far.<br /> <br /> If you have everyone within a bar for a very important court social event, with mortals and most of the Freehold &quot;encouraged&quot; to be there, do not have the owner of said bar use their powers to make one of the motley drugged/drunk and then push (not free) Goblin Wine onto said motley mate to make him even more drunk. Especially do not do this if the only interaction up until that point has been an intention to talk to learn a particular skill that Court excels in. It makes no sense in-universe, makes the character come off as malicious and the entire experience comes off as a roofie analogue. This goes in particular for Changelings, given the Gentry are going to be engaging in this kind of behavior <i>a lot</i>.<br /> <br /> Do not dismiss the above as a player character's &quot;weakness&quot;. The act performed there is a breach of Changeling etiquette, and is an insult to both the owner of the bar and the character, if not the court.<br /> <br /> If one player character has been unable to make any real contributions for the motley in the game, outside of humor from getting drunk, then do not deliberately hamper important contributions they make in favor of another character who has had a lot more prominence and or skill in the area. In particular, don't let that favored character's very presence hamper other players without noting and asking if the other players are okay with that. It will make that player character feel even less able and will net you, and said favored player, absolutely zero points with them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-09-04T15:58:53-07:00","id":1251513,"title":"","content":"<div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Taidragon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=1232056#post1232056\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Reviving this topic with a few examples from my most recent Changeling game. There were fun things done, but this dragged me out of the experience.<br /> <br /> If your players have established themselves as having a pretty important job for the freehold, such as hosting a halfway house for newly escaped Changelings, do not establish a very important plot where the players are working in place of one of the court monarchs. If you do, make sure that it does not need to be executed within a relatively tight schedule at the very beginning of the game. Let them invest time to their job, get a norm going, build up some ancillary skills, and <i>then </i>throw in things that they need to be concerned with on a tight schedule. Any invested in the job, if not built for it, will find themselves disconcerted when they're put out of their element, and considering everyone is only at Wyrd 1 at that point it will force them to focus their XP solely on preparing for said plot and not on actually developing their characters.<br /> <br /> Related, if you see/hear a player badgering another how to spend their own experiences, stop it. Some guidance is good, but outright telling someone how to spend it and making them question their own character is very much not okay. A person's character is their own creation. Suggestions are fine, but if you're going to take the reigns of their development then you are going way too far.<br /> <br /> If you have everyone within a bar for a very important court social event, with mortals and most of the Freehold &quot;encouraged&quot; to be there, do not have the owner of said bar use their powers to make one of the motley drugged/drunk and then push (not free) Goblin Wine onto said motley mate to make him even more drunk. Especially do not do this if the only interaction up until that point has been an intention to talk to learn a particular skill that Court excels in. It makes no sense in-universe, makes the character come off as malicious and the entire experience comes off as a roofie analogue. This goes in particular for Changelings, given the Gentry are going to be engaging in this kind of behavior <i>a lot</i>.<br /> <br /> Do not dismiss the above as a player character's &quot;weakness&quot;. The act performed there is a breach of Changeling etiquette, and is an insult to both the owner of the bar and the character, if not the court.<br /> <br /> If one player character has been unable to make any real contributions for the motley in the game, outside of humor from getting drunk, then do not deliberately hamper important contributions they make in favor of another character who has had a lot more prominence and or skill in the area. In particular, don't let that favored character's very presence hamper other players without noting and asking if the other players are okay with that. It will make that player character feel even less able and will net you, and said favored player, absolutely zero points with them.</div> </div> </div> </div></div></div> </div> </div><br /> Those were... very specific scenarios.<br /> <br /> A tip from my own game:<br /> If you want paranoia in your freehold to be a thing in your game, make sure to first reel your players in emotionally to that freehold. Make them like the place and feel <b>home</b>, safe and loyal to the freehold. I had a plot for a take-over by one of the... morally darker grey NPCs, but my players weren't completely invested in the freehold yet, so when things got worse... they left!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13128-spinnerling","name":"Spinnerling","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13128_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"34"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-11T13:12:03-07:00","id":1260179,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spinnerling</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=1251513#post1251513\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Those were... very specific scenarios.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, those were specific scenarios. That game upset me pretty badly, in part from one character whose player\u2019s actions grated on me (such as telling me I shouldn\u2019t take particular contracts and starting with Mantle 5) and the ST, who had us doing a quest in the interests of the Winter Queen for a Mage with a club on the moon that would have us doing a massive auction heist that would end in us stealing literal contracts being held in a bastion, all in less than a week in-game...when we\u2019d settled and started the game running a half-way house for escaped Changelings. We spent more game time in the Hedge than we did with our charges. And a character in literal sad clown make-up out-socialed my Fairest Playmate in talking a priest into letting the motley explore a church, when said Sad Clown\u2019s very presence doubled the penalty on the contested roll my character was making. It has been months since I left that game and I\u2019m still bitter about it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5803-taidragon","name":"Taidragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5803_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1038"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-15T08:52:21-07:00","id":1260679,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Spinnerling</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=1251513#post1251513\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Those were... very specific scenarios.<br /> <br /> A tip from my own game:<br /> If you want paranoia in your freehold to be a thing in your game, make sure to first reel your players in emotionally to that freehold. Make them like the place and feel <b>home</b>, safe and loyal to the freehold. I had a plot for a take-over by one of the... morally darker grey NPCs, but my players weren't completely invested in the freehold yet, so when things got worse... they left!</div> </div> </div> </div>This is pretty great thank you!<br /> <br /> I think this is the pitfall that I ran into when running my first CtL game. I didn't do enough to ingratiate the player's into the freehold before putting in the darker and more untrustworthy elements into the game. So when the Freehold was about to tear itself apart from within and without, the scenario didn't garner the emotional impact I was hopingfor simply because the Char's didn't truly care about the freehold itself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Frequent Story Teller for the Circle of Five gaming group.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/654-xiongrey","name":"xiongrey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar654_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"549"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-15T12:40:45-07:00","id":1260733,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=928563#post928563\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A thing which tangentially relates to Loki's GM's cannibalism gaffe and more directly relates to one of those common reading attitudes about the game:<br /> <br /> 2e's done a good job at making it more up-front, and Arc's old spiel about Never Going Back as the corollary to Finding Your Way Home is pretty direct about it, but it bears emphasis that <b>the Durance and its related traumas do not render the Lost immune to casual malice</b>.<br /> <br /> Even beyond the explicit &amp;quot;bad guy&amp;quot; narrative archetypes that the Wyrd can and does orchestrate, most changelings who've been around for a while are not going to be especially good people without deliberate effort. This is a group of people with multiple innate means of getting out of trouble and a natural bias toward a smaller-scale version of the same behavior as the beings that made them into magical-narrative actors that can talk to the whole world. The vast majority have learned better than to do anything for free, and no small number of them have deeply-ingrained agendas amplified by the loss of their emotional regulators.<br /> <br /> Put plainly, don't run changeling NPCs as simply humans who happen to be able to call on ancient pixie pacts, because that misses out on the ways that the Lost are fae.</div> </div> </div> </div>So Vriska serket is a Changeling","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-15T13:42:14-07:00","id":1260744,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Konradleijon</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=1260733#post1260733\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So Vriska serket is a Changeling</div> </div> </div> </div>Through certain lenses, yes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-15T15:30:09-07:00","id":1260760,"title":"","content":"Your a Homestuck! , and she seems to be like a perfect Changling always thinking in terms of a story during some really bad stuff like pushing Tavros off a cliff Etrc","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23952,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","user":"Konradleijon","date":". 10-15-2018, 06:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23952-konradleijon","name":"Konradleijon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23952_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1441"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-26T08:24:06-08:00","id":1276796,"title":"","content":"Give it to me straight. I am desperate for an actual Changeling experience of any sort and I'm edging on finally trying to GM. But I:<br /> <br /> 1) Have never run, planned or even helped someone run a game in any system<br /> 2) Have played around 5 sessions of Changeling 1e and/or Underhill version at most<br /> 3) Have not written any fiction of any sort since middle school<br /> 4) Have no acting or improv experience<br /> 5) Get easily distracted from any project that takes more than a few weeks<br /> 6) Am currently in college, working in computers, applying to other schools, writing for my school newspaper, and trying to find another internship closer to my major all at once<br /> 7) Am part of two different D&amp;D campaigns in 3.5e and 5e with my friends<br /> <br /> So: <b>Should I try to run Changeling: the Lost 2e when the full book comes out?</b>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>A god is just a monster you kneel to. </b>- ArcaneArts, Quoting &quot;Fall of Gods&quot; <div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/6.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7920-master-aquatosic","name":"Master Aquatosic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7920_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-26T11:30:50-08:00","id":1276834,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Master Aquatosic</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/927830-gming-changeling-what-not-to-do?p=1276796#post1276796\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Give it to me straight.<br /> <br /> ...<br /> <br /> <b>Should I try to run Changeling: the Lost 2e when the full book comes out?</b></div> </div> </div> </div>I say, why not? The only way to start getting better is to start in the first place. You seem have the desire and you care enough to ask questions before starting, likely to make the experience as fun for all parties as possible. So I say definitely!<br /> <br /> On your numbers:<br /> 1) Got to start somewhere <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />. I did basically the same when I first started.<br /> 2) That means you've got some experience in the setting <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> just seeing the feel of the setting is good.<br /> 3) While it's cool to have it's not really required of an ST. <br /> 4) Neither have I, in fact I'm really bad at the method RPing even as a player. That said, everyone at the table has fun when each player/ST comes together to tell eachother a story in their own words. No matter the delivery. <br /> 5) Just gotta try, really. Having players and a deadline usually helps me keep on track.<br /> 6) That's a lot. Sounds like planning or something on the side might help alliviate some stress.<br /> 7) Lots of story influences <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://www.kickstarter.com/projects/200664283/demon-the-descent-prestige-edition\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> Frequent Story Teller for the Circle of Five gaming group.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/654-xiongrey","name":"xiongrey","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar654_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"549"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-26T11:41:09-08:00","id":1276841,"title":"","content":"Thanks. It helps that the fairly well-liked villain in the 3.5 one is already a Fallen Angel who has declared herself Queen of the Fae","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>A god is just a monster you kneel to. </b>- ArcaneArts, Quoting &quot;Fall of Gods&quot; <div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/6.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7920-master-aquatosic","name":"Master Aquatosic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7920_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-12-26T14:41:16-08:00","id":1276874,"title":"","content":"The best advice I can give you is do it and don't be afraid of screwing it up. You're going to make mistakes; you're going to think of things you could have done better after it's too late.<br /> <br /> That's ok. It's ok for your early attempts to feel flat or off key. Those are learning experiences that will make your later games better. If you think of it as practice and not something that you have to be doing perfectly right now you'll be a whole lot more relaxed, which will make the game better for everyone. Just be honest with your players that you're new and they need to cut you some slack while you learn the ropes. If they're the kind of people you want at a gaming table they'll help you out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}